    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Loss of use coverage pays the extra cost of living somewhere else when a covered loss, such as a fire, a burst pipe or storm damage, makes your home unlivable. It does not replace your normal budget. It covers the <em>increase</em> over what you would have spent at home, up to a limit that varies by policy.</p>
<p>This guide breaks down how loss of use coverage works for Texas homeowners and renters: the two main parts of it, what typically counts and what does not, how limits work, and how to document expenses so you actually get reimbursed.</p>

<h2>What Is Loss of Use Coverage?</h2>
<p>On most homeowners policies, loss of use is listed as its own coverage, often labeled Coverage D. It sits alongside dwelling coverage (the structure), other structures and personal property. Your declarations page shows the limit.</p>
<p>The Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) explains that most home, renters and condo policies pay some additional living expenses if you cannot live in your home while your insurer is paying to repair it. The key condition is that the damage must come from an event your policy covers. If the cause of loss is excluded, loss of use is excluded too.</p>
<p>Loss of use usually has two pieces:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Additional living expenses (ALE):</strong> the increase in your living costs so your household can maintain its normal standard of living while displaced.</li>
<li><strong>Fair rental value:</strong> the rent you lose if part of your home that you rent to others, such as a garage apartment or a rented room, becomes unlivable. It is generally reduced by expenses that stop while the space cannot be rented.</li>
</ul>
<p>Policy forms differ in wording, so read the exact loss of use section in your own policy rather than relying on a generic description.</p>

<h2>"Additional" Means the Increase Over Normal Costs</h2>
<p>This is the single most misunderstood part of ALE. Your insurer does not pay your total cost of living while you are displaced. It pays the difference between what you are spending now and what you normally spend.</p>
<p>A few examples make the idea concrete:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Housing:</strong> if you normally pay a mortgage and now also pay for a hotel, the hotel is a new cost. The mortgage is not, because you would pay it anyway.</li>
<li><strong>Food:</strong> if your family usually spends a set amount on groceries but must eat out because the hotel has no kitchen, the covered amount is the extra cost above your normal grocery bill, not the full restaurant tab.</li>
<li><strong>Utilities:</strong> if utilities at the damaged house are shut off, that saving can reduce what the insurer owes. If you keep paying them and also pay utilities at a rental, the rental utilities may count.</li>
<li><strong>Commuting:</strong> if your temporary housing is farther from work or school, the added mileage is the covered part.</li>
</ul>
<p>Expect the adjuster to ask what your normal monthly spending looks like. Bank and card statements from the months before the loss are the easiest way to show a baseline.</p>

<h2>What Loss of Use Coverage Typically Pays For</h2>
<p>TDI lists hotels, eating out, laundry and renting an apartment during repairs as examples of additional living expenses. Depending on your policy and circumstances, reasonable covered costs often include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Hotel stays or a short-term furnished rental</li>
<li>Temporary rent for a house or apartment during longer repairs</li>
<li>The extra cost of restaurant meals over your normal grocery spending</li>
<li>Added mileage or transportation to work and school</li>
<li>Laundromat or laundry service costs you would not have at home</li>
<li>Pet boarding, if your temporary housing does not allow pets</li>
<li>Furniture rental for an unfurnished temporary home</li>
<li>Moving and storage costs tied to the displacement</li>
</ul>
<p>The standard is "reasonable." A comparable rental for your family size in a similar area is usually reasonable. A luxury suite when you live in a modest three-bedroom house may be questioned. When in doubt, ask your adjuster before you sign a lease or book an extended stay.</p>

<h2>What Loss of Use Usually Does Not Cover</h2>
<p>Several situations commonly fall outside ALE, and knowing them prevents surprises.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Costs you would have anyway:</strong> mortgage payments, car payments, insurance premiums and normal groceries.</li>
<li><strong>Damage from an excluded cause:</strong> if the loss is not covered, neither is loss of use. Standard homeowners policies exclude flood, for example.</li>
<li><strong>Power outages with no damage:</strong> TDI says that if you leave because of a power outage and your home was not damaged, your policy will not cover ALE.</li>
<li><strong>NFIP flood claims:</strong> TDI notes that National Flood Insurance Program policies do not pay additional living expenses.</li>
<li><strong>Coastal evacuations:</strong> TDI says Texas Windstorm Insurance Association policies may include ALE only for a primary residence, and the TWIA endorsement excludes costs from a voluntary or mandatory evacuation.</li>
<li><strong>Lease cancellation:</strong> many policy forms exclude loss or expense from canceling a lease or agreement.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Civil Authority Coverage</h2>
<p>Sometimes your home is fine, but officials will not let you in because of damage nearby. Many homeowners policies include a civil authority provision for this. In common policy forms, if a civil authority prohibits you from using your home because of direct damage to neighboring property from a covered peril, the policy pays ALE and fair rental value for a limited period, often no more than two weeks.</p>
<p>Not every policy includes this language, and the time limit varies by form. Civil authority coverage also generally requires damage to nearby property from a covered peril. A precautionary evacuation order before a storm hits, with no damage, usually does not qualify. Check your policy's loss of use section for the exact wording.</p>

<h2>Limits and How Long Loss of Use Lasts</h2>
<p>Loss of use limits vary by insurer and policy form. According to TDI, most policies set the limit at 10% to 20% of what the house is insured for. Using TDI's example, a 10% limit on a home insured for $200,000 gives $20,000 of coverage.</p>
<p>TDI also says coverage usually lasts up to 12 months or until you use up the limit, whichever comes first. Many policy forms phrase the time element as the shortest time needed to repair or replace the home or, if you permanently relocate, the shortest time for your household to settle elsewhere. If you reach the limit before repairs are done, you cover the rest yourself.</p>
<p>That makes repair delays expensive. If contractor scheduling, permitting or a claim dispute stretches the timeline, track how fast your ALE is being used and tell your adjuster early. Disputes over the repair amount can sometimes be resolved through your policy's <a href="/blog/appraisal-clause-insurance/">appraisal clause</a>.</p>

<h2>Homeowners vs. Renters</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Issue</th><th>Homeowner</th><th>Renter</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Where coverage comes from</td><td>Loss of use section of the homeowners policy</td><td>Loss of use section of the renters policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Normal housing cost that continues</td><td>Mortgage payment (not covered)</td><td>Rent, if the lease still requires it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Covered housing amount</td><td>Temporary housing cost, since it is new</td><td>Generally the increase over normal rent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fair rental value</td><td>Possible if part of the home is rented to others</td><td>Usually not applicable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Landlord's role</td><td>Not applicable</td><td>Landlord's policy covers the building, not your ALE</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Renters should read their lease too. If the lease ends rent obligations when the unit becomes unlivable, your normal rent stops, which changes the math on what is "additional."</p>

<h2>Worked Example: Calculating Additional Living Expenses</h2>
<p>Suppose a kitchen fire forces a Houston family of four out of their home for one month. Their normal monthly costs and their costs while displaced look like this. These figures are illustrative only.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Expense</th><th>Normal monthly cost</th><th>Cost while displaced</th><th>Possible ALE</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Mortgage</td><td>$2,100</td><td>$2,100</td><td>$0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary furnished rental</td><td>$0</td><td>$3,000</td><td>$3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Food</td><td>$900</td><td>$1,500</td><td>$600</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commute mileage</td><td>$150</td><td>$275</td><td>$125</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laundry</td><td>$0</td><td>$80</td><td>$80</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pet boarding</td><td>$0</td><td>$450</td><td>$450</td></tr>
<tr><td>Utilities at the damaged home</td><td>$300</td><td>$0 (shut off)</td><td>-$300</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>$3,450</strong></td><td><strong>$7,405</strong></td><td><strong>$3,955</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The family spent $7,405 that month, but the likely ALE claim is about $3,955: the increase over their normal $3,450. How an insurer treats each line depends on the policy wording and the adjuster, so treat this as a way to organize your numbers, not a promise of payment.</p>

<h2>How to Document and Get Paid</h2>
<p>TDI's advice is short: save all receipts to show your company. In practice, a little more structure speeds things up.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Call your insurer before committing to long-term housing.</strong> Ask what daily or monthly housing amount it considers reasonable.</li>
<li><strong>Ask about an advance.</strong> Many insurers will advance part of ALE so you are not floating hotel bills on a credit card.</li>
<li><strong>Keep every receipt.</strong> Hotel folios, rental agreements, restaurant receipts, mileage logs, laundry and boarding invoices.</li>
<li><strong>Record your baseline.</strong> Pull statements from the months before the loss to show normal food, utility and commuting costs.</li>
<li><strong>Submit regularly.</strong> Send expenses every week or two with a simple spreadsheet instead of one large stack at the end.</li>
<li><strong>Log every conversation.</strong> Date, name and what was approved, then confirm approvals by email.</li>
</ol>
<p>[[product:document-scanner]]</p>
<p>Scanning receipts the day you get them keeps faded thermal paper from becoming a dispute later. For the full claim process from first call to final payment, see our step-by-step guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-file-a-home-insurance-claim/">how to file a home insurance claim</a>, and if a fire caused the loss, our <a href="/blog/what-to-do-after-a-house-fire/">house fire recovery checklist</a> covers the first days in detail. Policy language controls every claim, so for large or disputed ALE claims, confirm your rights with a licensed public adjuster or Texas insurance attorney.</p>

<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Loss of use coverage keeps a covered disaster from also wrecking your budget, but it only pays the increase over your normal costs, only for covered damage and only up to your limit. Check your declarations page now so you know the number, keep receipts from day one, show your normal spending baseline and talk to your adjuster before you sign a lease.</p>
